    {
      "id": "n1",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "title": "går, i morgen, på søndag",
      "text": "\"Går\" is \"go\" or \"walk\": jeg går, du går, hun går, Anna går. \"Går i kirka\" is the usual way to say you go to church; \"går med\" means \"come along\". \"I morgen\" is \"tomorrow\" and \"på søndag\" is \"on Sunday\". Days of the week begin with a small letter in Norwegian: søndag.",
      "explains": [
        "nb.gaa-place",
        "nb.time-days",
        "nb.lowercase"
      ],
      "afterScenes": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "n2",
      "kind": "usage",
      "title": "Jeg tror på Gud",
      "text": "\"Tro\" means \"believe\" and also \"think\": \"Jeg tror på Gud\" is \"I believe in God\", with \"på\" before what you believe in. Gud, like a name, starts with a capital letter. \"Jeg tror …\" can also mean \"I think …\"; the sentence shows which.",
      "explains": [
        "nb.tro"
      ],
